# Seat: Interaction and states — Round 7 (working notes, written as I go)

Build: commit 984a0f17, frozen. Read the 119 ok() assertions in
interaction-check.mjs and the paid-for defect library first; both are
excluded from my findings.

## Log

- Tab order @1440 tasks.board: 18 stops, roving at rail (1 stop), view
  switcher (1 stop), card grid (1 stop + 4 in-card controls), Add row.
  Arrows walk all 5 lanes and every card; the empty `waiting` lane is
  skipped rather than trapping. No console errors. Home/End are no-ops
  inside the card rover (minor, not filed).
- `.cardDots` ("Move this task") is opacity:0 at rest, revealed by
  :hover/:focus-within, forced to opacity 1 under `pointer: coarse`, and
  its slot is reserved in layout so revealing it moves nothing. This
  class is closed — not a finding.
- Carry model verified live: Space announces "Picked up …", arrows
  announce "In progress, position 1 of 4.", Escape cancels and announces
  "Move cancelled. … is back in the to do column." and the card really
  returns to `todo`. This is award-level; nothing to file.
- CANDIDATE (parked): `timeline.loading` reserves a 372x372 card and the
  arrival is 372x1653 at 390, 1242x854 at 1440. The skeleton draws the
  horizon head only — no reserve at all for the measure, which is the
  organising object. Not a declared state and not shot, so weighed for
  scope before filing.

## FINDING 1 — seam-send-dead-and-swapped (CONFIRMED, headline)
`[data-app="notes"] [data-act="send"]`, the seam's primary act.

Clear the wording field (`.peelField`) — the natural retype gesture — and
three things happen at once at every desk width:

1. `_gate-suite.html:19388` runs
   `send.toggleAttribute("aria-disabled", !ready)`. `toggleAttribute`
   writes the EMPTY STRING. `aria-disabled=""` is an invalid ARIA token,
   so Chromium falls back to the default (false). Playwright's a11y
   snapshot returns `{"role":"button","name":"Write the wording, then
   send"}` — no disabled state at all. First paint is correct
   (`:18076` emits `aria-disabled="true"`); only the live path lies.
2. Because `""` != `"true"`, every guard keyed on
   `[aria-disabled="true"]` stops matching: `.verb/.headAct` ink-3
   colour (5680-5682) and `cursor: default` (5683). Measured: bg stays
   `rgb(17,17,17)`, colour `rgb(255,255,255)`, `cursor: pointer`,
   `opacity 1`, `pointer-events: auto`. It is a full-ink primary pill.
3. Click it or press Enter on it: 13 cards before, 13 after; `#say` is
   silent; peel stays open; focus stays put. A silent dead control on the
   one act the suite exists to perform.

And the label swap re-wraps the footer. Measured send rect
@1440: [596,591,138,36] -> [272,635,227,36] (324px left, 44px down,
138->227 wide). "Never mind" goes [491,591] -> [637,591] — 146px RIGHT,
landing INSIDE the rectangle Send occupied a frame earlier (right edges
both at x=734). True at 768, 1280, 1440 and 1920. So the person who
select-alls and deletes to retype, then reaches back to where Send was,
presses the control that throws the peel away.

FIX: at :19388 use
`send.setAttribute("aria-disabled", ready ? "false" : "true")`, and stop
swapping the button's own text — keep it "Send to Tasks" at its 138px
width and move "Write the wording, then send" under `.peelField` as the
field's hint, so the footer row never rewraps and "Never mind" never
slides into the slot the primary act just left.
COST: 0.4

## FINDING 2 — across-editor-opens-below-the-fold (CONFIRMED)
`[data-app="timeline"] .b-edit` on the ACROSS desk (the default layout).

Round 6 moved the editor out from between the horizon and the track so
the grab handles stop being pushed off screen. It now opens AFTER the
plan — and lands below the fold. Measured, on the `.app.sheet` band,
after a full 1.4s settle:

| entry | viewport | .b-edit rect | px of editor visible | steppers on screen |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `.b-grab` | 1440x960  | [196,859,1130,554] | 101 | 0 of 4 |
| `.b-grab` | 1440x1000 | [196,899,1130,554] | 101 | 0 of 4 |
| `.b-grab` | 1920x1000 | [196,899,1610,554] | 101 | 0 of 4 |
| `.b-grab` | 1280x900  | [196,895, 970,554] | **5** | 0 of 4 |
| `[data-act="add"]` | 1440x1000 | [196,972,1130,554] | 28 | 0 of 4 |

The first stepper sits 146-242px BELOW the viewport bottom in every one
of those. Screenshot at 1280x900: pressing "Edit Send the invitations"
paints a faint wash behind that one label on the measure and scrolls the
sheet 173px — so the person's place moves and the thing they opened is
still not on screen. Keyboard users recover on the next Tab (native
focus scroll); pointer users get nothing at all.

`keepInBand` (:21926) is the mechanism and it is doing what it says:
it keeps the MOMENT'S ROW in the band. Nothing ever puts `.b-edit` in
the band. The behaviour gate cannot see this — `across @w · placing a
moment is not done blind` (:875) asserts `.b-grab` handles remain
visible, never the editor.

The DOWN layout is correct: [227,615,344,554], 345px visible, 4 of 4
steppers on screen.

FIX: in `keepInBand`, when `.b-edit` is present and the band resolved to
the `.app.sheet` scroller (the `!fixed && !paneScrolls` branch), run the
existing minimum-correction arithmetic on the PANEL rather than the
moment's row: `top = panel.getBoundingClientRect().top -
box.getBoundingClientRect().top`, `height =
panel.getBoundingClientRect().height`. Verified by hand: scrolling the
band so `.b-edit`'s bottom sits 24px inside the viewport puts all 4
steppers on screen and still leaves 6 of 7 grab handles visible at
1280x1000 and 7 of 7 at 1440/1920x1000 — comfortably over the gate's
`>= 1`.
COST: 0.4
- Filters: the overdue chip states itself ("Showing 1 overdue task. 12
  others are hidden."), keeps head counts honest, and RELEASES ITSELF
  when moot with a spoken reason ("Nothing is overdue, so the filter is
  off."). The dead-end-filter class from the library is closed.
- Dense tick at scrollTop 251: source lane keeps 251, focus follows the
  card to Done and stays on screen, undo restores both. Clean. (Undo
  does leave the Done lane's own trayBody at 145 having started at 0 —
  drift in a lane nobody is looking at; too small to file.)

## FINDING 3 — notes-drops-focus-at-720 (CONFIRMED)
`[data-app="notes"]`, the 720 crossing.

Focus a Notes control, narrow past 720, widen back. Measured, on one
page narrowed and widened (the gate's own method):

| control | exists @390 | 1440 | 390 | back to 1440 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `[data-act="review"]` `.idxGo` | yes | held | **body** | **body** |
| `[data-act="photo"]` (`.verb`->`.dockGlyph`) | yes | held | **body** | **body** |
| `[data-act="send"]` (seam primary) | yes | held | **body** | **body** |
| `.topField` (a TEXTAREA) | yes | held | held | held |

Every one of those controls is present and visible on both sides of the
crossing. The next Tab from body restarts at the top of the document
(the first note row). Nothing announces the loss.

Tasks holds focus across the same crossing in both directions (checked
on `[data-id="demo-t-01"]` and on the dock search field). Timeline moves
it to a real heading. Notes alone drops it on the floor.

Cause is exact: Notes' repaint (~:19100) captures the index scroll, the
read-body scroll, and a CARET — but only
`if (active.tagName === "TEXTAREA" || active.tagName === "INPUT")`.
`mount.innerHTML = ...` at :19143 then destroys every button. The
comment above it promises "Nothing here may cost the operator their
place"; it keeps that promise for fields only. Same-band resizes
(1440->1400->1300) and the 1199 crossing all hold focus, so this is the
720 repaint specifically.

The gate cannot see it: `${product} · crossing 720 both ways throws
nothing` (:1467) asserts layout, toggles, lanes, rows, sideways scroll
and console noise — never `document.activeElement`. "focus survives the
repaint" exists only in the pattern comment at :212.

FIX: in the same capture block, when `active` is not a field, record
`{ act: active.dataset.act, role: active.getAttribute("aria-label") }`
— both are stable across the breakpoint — and after `mount.innerHTML`
is written, re-find it with
`mount.querySelector('[data-act="'+act+'"]')`, confirm the aria-label
matches, and `.focus({ preventScroll: true })`. This is the two-key
selector-then-role rule Notes already runs for fields, extended from
fields to controls.
COST: 0.2

## FINDING 4 — review-confirm-is-not-one (CONFIRMED)
`[data-app="notes"]` review, the "Delete it everywhere?" two-step
confirm (`.confirm` / `[data-act="d-delete-yes"|"d-delete-no"]`).

Press Delete on a review card. The confirm comes up, `#say` reads
"Delete this note? … You can undo this for 30 seconds.", focus lands on
"Keep it". Escape, Enter and Space all behave. Then, with the confirm
still up:

| key | what happens |
|---|---|
| `t` | confirm gone with no word; the SEND peel opens; focus jumps into the wording textarea; the pending delete is dropped silently |
| `k` | confirm gone with no word; the note is FILED and the queue advances 1 of 8 -> 2 of 8: "Kept in Notes. 7 left." |
| `l` | confirm gone with no word; "Left for later", queue moves to the next note |
| `d` | correctly a no-op |
| `Escape` / `Enter` / `Space` | correctly cancel, "Nothing was deleted." |

So the person who asked to delete a note and then touched one letter has
committed a DIFFERENT decision on that same note and moved on, with
nothing said about the delete they asked for. The source comment at
:20166 says "Delete stays keycap-free behind its two-step confirm" — the
confirm does not hold the keyboard, so it isn't one.

Also: the confirm carries `role="status"`, not `role="alertdialog"`, has
no `aria-modal`, and Tab from "Keep it" walks straight out of it — index
row, dock search, account tile, body, then round to the rail — 8 stops
without ever returning to "Delete"/"Keep it".

FIX: `confirming` (:18634) is already the flag. In the keydown handler,
immediately after `if (typing) return;` (:20153) add
`if (state === "review" && confirming) return;` — that is above the `/`
search branch, the t/k/l branch (:20161) and the
`["ArrowDown","ArrowUp","j","k"]` branch (:20187), so a pending
destructive confirm owns the keyboard and only Escape and the two
answers' own Enter/Space reach anything. Then give the `.confirm` span
`role="alertdialog" aria-modal="true"` with an accessible name and keep
Tab cycling between its two buttons.
COST: 0.2

## FINDING 5 — two-popups-advertise-a-keyboard-they-do-not-have (CONFIRMED)
`[data-act="projects"]` (the project switcher) and `[data-rail="more"]`
(the spine's More menu) — both SUITE-level objects.

Four popups in this one application, driven identically (focus the
opener, Enter, ArrowDown, ArrowDown, Escape) at 1440x960:

| popup | role / items | focus on open | ArrowDown | Escape leaves focus on |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Show (Tasks head) | menu / 14 | IN the menu | walks the menu | "Show" — the word that opened it |
| card Move | menu / 5 | IN the menu | walks the menu | the card |
| **project switcher** | **listbox / 4 options** | on the opener | **nothing at all** | the opener |
| **rail More** | **menu / 3** | on the opener | **walks the RAIL behind the open menu** — "Settings", then "Orla" | "Orla, venue manager" — a rail tile, not the opener |

- The project switcher ships `aria-haspopup="listbox"`, flips
  `aria-expanded` to "true", and paints a real `role="listbox"`
  aria-label "Projects" with four `role="option"`s — and then has no
  listbox keyboard model: no `aria-activedescendant`, no
  `aria-controls`, arrows and Home/End all dead. Its options are
  reachable ONLY by Tab. This is the library's "keyboard models
  advertised but not implemented" class, verbatim.
- The rail's More menu declares `role="menu"` and the rail's own roving
  handler eats the arrows, so ArrowDown inside an open menu moves the
  rail tile UNDER the menu. Escape then abandons focus on whatever tile
  the rover reached. Show does this correctly two objects away.
- Separately, Tab from inside the open card Move menu lands on
  `**BODY**` while `.cardMenu` is still painted over the board; a second
  Tab reaches the rail.

FIX: give both objects the model Show already has, since it is written
and proven in this same file. For the switcher: on Enter/Space/ArrowDown
move focus to the selected `[role="option"]` (or set
`aria-activedescendant` on the opener and add `aria-controls`), let
Up/Down/Home/End walk the options, Enter choose, Escape close and return
focus to the opener. For rail More: while `moreOpen` is true the rail's
roving arrow handler must return early and hand Up/Down to the three
`.moreItem` buttons, with Escape returning focus to `[data-rail="more"]`.
For the card menu: close `.cardMenu` on Tab and move focus to the
control after `[data-act="menu"]` rather than to the body.
COST: 0.3

